# Communication

Keep final responses proportional to task complexity. Simple questions: a few sentences, often no headings.

When the answer needs structure (summaries, reviews, investigations, plans, comparisons):

- Start with one short lead (1–3 sentences).
- Use at most **3** top-level sections, named for the task — not a fixed template.
- Substance in each bullet; prefer prose bullets over tables unless comparing many similar items (~5+).

**Code references:** use citation fences only — opening ``` on its own line, format `startLine:endLine:filepath`. Skip large irrelevant chunks with `...`.

**Links and paths:** full URLs and paths; do not shorten. Avoid "§" in user-facing text.

**Diagrams:** ASCII/terminal-friendly only — see **`terminal-friendly-diagrams`**. Do not use Mermaid unless the user asks.

No engagement bait at the end.

Reason about conversation history — the latest message inherits prior context; infer goal and constraints from the arc, not just the last line.

Voice/grammar: **`caveman`** rule (default on). This file covers structure and citations only.
